---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FailClosedSecurityGate from "../../components/diagrams/FailClosedSecurityGate.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Enforcement Runtime"
  description="BeaconGuard runtime authorization for Request Admission and Result-to-Action: fail-closed ALLOW or DENY, provider separation, decision evidence, and evidence-before-effect where configured."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Enforcement Runtime</h1>

    <p>
      The BeaconGuard runtime authorization domain evaluates trusted context
      against the <strong>Active Verified Release</strong> and produces a
      Deterministic Decision Result—<strong>ALLOW</strong> or <strong>DENY</strong>—at
      configured authorization points in a governed AI transaction.
    </p>

    <p>It is designed to be:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Deterministic</li>
      <li>Fail closed where enforcement is configured</li>
      <li>Separable from management-plane playbook lifecycle</li>
      <li>Auditable by construction via Verifiable Decision Records</li>
    </ul>

    <FailClosedSecurityGate />

    <h2>Role in the System</h2>

    <p>
      Enforcement runtime covers <strong>Request Admission Authorization</strong>
      before AI provider invocation and <strong>Result-to-Action Authorization</strong>
      for a declared intended enterprise action. AI-generated results remain
      untrusted proposals. Provider success is not enterprise authorization.
      BeaconGuard authorizes; the enterprise application executes.
    </p>

    <p>It does not:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Modify prompts or rewrite application payloads as a product function</li>
      <li>Inspect model internals</li>
      <li>Perform heuristic or probabilistic authorization</li>
      <li>Authorize model-output content as semantic response scoring</li>
      <li>Replace systems of record or workflow applications</li>
      <li>Treat human review as a permission to execute</li>
      <li>Treat reports or AI-assisted insights as runtime authority</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Optional System Connector</h3>

    <p>
      Some applications do not natively produce the required request format. The
      optional <strong>System Connector</strong> maps source protocols and context
      into the required structure before Request Validation and Context
      Normalization.
    </p>

    <p>
      The System Connector does not evaluate controls, activate releases, or
      bypass enforcement.
    </p>

    <h2>Request Admission</h2>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Request Validation and Context Normalization</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Trust, Freshness, and Replay Checks</strong> where configured</li>
      <li><strong>Deterministic Playbook Evaluation</strong> against the Active Verified Release</li>
      <li><strong>Determine ALLOW or DENY</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Persist Decision Record</strong> (Verifiable Decision Record)</li>
      <li><strong>Apply Effect</strong> — ALLOW may permit provider invocation; DENY hard-stops with no provider invocation</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>AI Provider Interaction</h2>

    <p>
      After admission ALLOW, the enterprise application may invoke the AI
      provider. The provider is a separate trust boundary. The returned
      AI-generated result—including tool or action proposals—is an untrusted
      proposal requiring new governed authorization for any declared intended
      enterprise action.
    </p>

    <p>
      Approving a proposal does not resume the original AI response. Using the
      AI response as authorization is prohibited and not implemented.
    </p>

    <h2>Result-to-Action Authorization</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>Bind the AI-generated result to governed transaction context</li>
      <li>Enterprise application declares and normalizes the Intended Enterprise Action</li>
      <li>Deterministic Playbook Evaluation against the Active Verified Release</li>
      <li>Determine ALLOW or DENY</li>
      <li>Persist Decision Record</li>
      <li>ALLOW permits the enterprise application to execute the authorized action; DENY hard-stops with no enterprise effect</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Do not claim identical playbooks, identical latency, or separately deployed
      gateways at both points unless specifically verified for a given
      deployment.
    </p>

    <h2>Correlation and Evidence</h2>

    <p>
      Related decision and evidence identifiers support investigation of the
      request–result–action relationship. Correlation supports Verify and audit
      review; it is not presented as a named distributed correlator product.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where enforcement requires it, evidence is recorded before upstream forward
      (admission path) or before enterprise effect (action path). Exact
      evidence-before-effect ordering is mode- and configuration-dependent.
    </p>

    <h2>Runtime Source of Truth</h2>

    <p>
      At evaluation time, the <strong>Active Verified Release</strong> is the source
      of truth for control semantics. Management-plane playbook drafts and
      unsigned artifacts do not govern runtime decisions until they pass Release
      Verification and Activation.
    </p>

    <h2>Decision Contract</h2>

    <div class="docs-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Result</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>ALLOW (admission)</td>
            <td>Required controls are satisfied; after evidence requirements are met, provider invocation may proceed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>DENY (admission)</td>
            <td>Provider invocation is prevented; no AI provider call occurs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>ALLOW (action)</td>
            <td>Required controls are satisfied; after evidence requirements are met, the enterprise application may execute the authorized action.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>DENY (action)</td>
            <td>Enterprise effect is prevented; no authorized action execution occurs.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      When required approval is absent, BeaconGuard returns DENY with a
      deterministic reason indicating that approval is required. An illustrative
      reason style is <code>APPROVAL_REQUIRED</code>. Any governance or human-review
      obligation remains separate from the runtime permission result.
    </p>

    <h2>Failure Semantics</h2>

    <p>The runtime operates under <strong>fail-closed</strong> semantics where enforcement is configured.</p>

    <p>If any of the following occur:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Active Verified Release cannot be verified or is not activated</li>
      <li>Required artifacts or trust signals are missing or invalid</li>
      <li>Approved-pathway status is missing or invalid when required</li>
      <li>Freshness or replay checks fail</li>
      <li>Input normalization fails</li>
      <li>Internal evaluation error occurs</li>
      <li>Required shared replay state or evidence dependency cannot be verified</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The runtime must:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Return <strong>DENY</strong></li>
      <li>Emit a Verifiable Decision Record describing the failure category</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Silent fallback or permissive behavior is prohibited.</p>

    <h2>External Dependencies and Separation</h2>

    <p>Explicit external dependencies reinforce enforcement without becoming alternate evaluators:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Customer identity provider and workload identity</li>
      <li>Evidence provider</li>
      <li>Replay / shared security state where configured</li>
      <li>Key and secret custody</li>
      <li>Operational monitoring</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Keep the <strong>AI provider</strong> and the <strong>enterprise system of
      record</strong> as separate trust boundaries. Do not collapse them into a
      single “downstream system.”
    </p>

    <h2>Evidence Emission</h2>

    <p>
      For every evaluation the runtime emits integrity-protected decision
      evidence designed for replay and verification. Records preserve release
      identity, playbook identity, decision context, check outcomes, and the
      ALLOW or DENY result. Storage durability and append-only behavior depend
      on the configured evidence provider and shared customer deployment
      responsibilities.
    </p>

